  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a mid‑infrared sensing device based on a Te₂As₃Se₅ optical fiber integrated into a battery‑like layered structure. Mid‑infrared light enters the fiber from the left and exits on the right as a transmitted spectrum with several absorption dips, indicating interaction with surrounding chemical species.\n\nThe central cylindrical assembly contains alternating layers identified as positive electrode, negative electrode, and separator, with epoxy resin at the ends. A simple circuit symbol below suggests the device operates while electrically connected, likely during electrochemical activity. The magnified inset shows how light propagates through the chalcogenide fiber by repeated internal reflections. At each reflection, an evanescent wave extends beyond the fiber surface into the nearby medium, allowing molecules outside the fiber to absorb part of the infrared signal. Small molecular models around the fiber emphasize this sensing mechanism.\n\nKey optical parameters are sketched in the inset, including the incidence angle, penetration depth of the evanescent field, refractive indices, and a fiber diameter marked as about 150 μm. Overall, the image conveys an operando fiber‑optic approach for monitoring chemical changes inside an electrochemical cell through mid‑infrared evanescent‑wave spectroscopy.",
